This is a major AWS DevOps concept.

7. 💰 Reserved Instances

Reserved Instances are basically a longer-term pricing commitment for eligible EC2 usage.

Instead of paying the normal flexible rate, you commit to a term and can get a discount.

Think:

On-demand
= Pay normally

Reserved
= Commit longer
= Lower effective cost

Useful when you know you'll need capacity for a long time.

8. Spot Requests

Spot Instance = Cheap EC2 capacity using spare AWS capacity.

You can get a large discount, but AWS can interrupt the instance when it needs the capacity back.

Good for:

Batch processing
Testing
Data processing
Temporary workloads

Not ideal for:

Critical production database
9. Savings Plans

Another way to reduce AWS compute costs by committing to a certain level of usage/spend.

Simple idea:

Commit to AWS usage
        ↓
Get discounted pricing

Savings Plans are broader/flexible compared with traditional Reserved Instances.

10. Dedicated Hosts

A Dedicated Host = Physical AWS server dedicated to you.

Normally:

Physical Server
 ├── Customer A
 ├── Customer B
 └── Customer C

Dedicated Host:

Physical Server
       |
       └── YOUR workloads

Used when you have specific licensing/compliance requirements.

11. Capacity Reservations

This reserves EC2 capacity in a specific Availability Zone.

Example:

You need to guarantee that you can launch:

5 EC2 instances

AWS reserves capacity for you.

Important distinction:

Capacity Reservation = Reserve capacity

Reserved Instance = Pricing commitment
